Implement a quantum oracle on $*N*$ qubits which implements the following function:

![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/b2eb3d0e407b200708fe5e4dc65e777bc2c88984.png)

Here ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/b27ec8b8056386de828da0df174348afa7d29512.png) (a vector of $*N*$ integers, each of which can be 0 or 1), and ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/823bed8a1021f190960cf0b721fad709b7641d0e.png) is a vector of $*N*$ 1s.

For an explanation on how this type of quantum oracles works, see [Introduction to quantum oracles](https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/60319).

You have to implement an operation which takes the following inputs:

- an array of $*N*$ qubits $*x*$ in arbitrary state (input register), $1 ≤ *N* ≤ 8$,
- a qubit $*y*$ in arbitrary state (output qubit),
- an array of $*N*$ integers $*b*$, representing the vector ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/71fa108edb785ca9f729fa3cd5ad18556dd682e4.png). Each element of $*b*$ will be 0 or 1.

The operation doesn't have an output; its "output" is the state in which it leaves the qubits.

Your code should have the following signature:

```
namespace Solution {
    open Microsoft.Quantum.Primitive;
    open Microsoft.Quantum.Canon;

    operation Solve (x : Qubit[], y : Qubit, b : Int[]) : ()
    {
        body
        {
